---

Critical Technical Decisions

1. Real-time vs. Async turns: For D&D, I recommend async turns with timeout (e.g., 2 min per turn) rather than requiring all players online simultaneously. Players can submit actions when ready.

2. DM AI costs: With 4 players, your OpenAI costs 4x. Your compression system becomes essential — keep it, but add per-campaign caching so the same compressed context serves all party members.

3. State authority: One player should be the "Campaign Host" with save/load privileges. Others are participants. This maps to your existing atomic operation pattern.

4. Module ownership: Currently modules are local files. In multiplayer, the host's module selection determines the campaign world. Other players download module metadata on join.

